+## 197.10 - (Improved / tuned) An era-progressive offensive production floor preserves early AI strategies while correcting weak middle and late armies
+
+Screenshots/files for this issue: [google drive folder link](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1_sNxrNx7x6ZykMifqetc2flHLcivMmRK?usp=sharing).
+
+Since KI#197.9's original investigation and flat-40 conclusion, AdvCiv-SAS has reworked civilization-specific buildings and made other balance changes that alter production opportunity costs. For example, Castle cost was increased from 110 to 130 because 110 was too cheap for its value. The earlier save-file 460 findings remain the historical evidence and reasoning for that stage of development, but their preferred numeric tuning is not assumed to remain optimal under the later rules. This follow-up therefore builds on KI#197.9 rather than rewriting its past conclusion.
+
+KI#197.9's flat 40% offensive production-capacity floor substantially improved the previously low mid/late military output, but the same pressure also applied during the Ancient, Classical and Medieval eras. Early production has especially large opportunity costs because a Granary, Worker, Settler or economic building can compound for most of the game, and early conquest against a sufficiently strong rival is riskier than later expansion with a mature economy and army. A healthy AI can also choose military production through its ordinary personality, war, danger, composition and strategic logic even when this floor is low. The floor is only a minimum, not a unit-production cap; lowering it therefore preserves more peaceful and economic strategies without preventing an AI from building an army when its normal situation calls for one.
+
+The flat target was split into seven independently XML-tunable Ancient, Classical, Medieval, Renaissance, Industrial, Modern and Future percentages. This deliberately allows non-linear policies: players can keep early freedom and raise later military pressure, retain a flat value, or even prefer more early and less late pressure. The seven values are cached together and selected by current era, so the flexibility has negligible runtime cost. Appended custom eras safely reuse the Future value; a mod-mod that inserts or reorders eras would need to adapt the ordered table to its era layout.
+
+### Save file 461: initial comparative tuning
+
+Primary comparative benchmark: **save file 461**, using the same starting save with BBAI military-production level 3 and SASGameRecord level 3. Unless stated otherwise, aggregate figures below include the 11 civilizations and exclude the Barbarian player. The broad flat baselines established the trade-off clearly:
+
+| Flat floor | Offensive units produced by T350 | Buildings completed by T350 | Civilization population at T350 | Combined research rate at T350 | Production lost in combat by T350 | Result |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| 0% | 638 | 1,206 | 1,004 | 3,436 | 102,953 | T392 Space |
+| 20% | 947 | 1,302 | 1,063 | 4,729 | 78,343 | T373 Space |
+| 40% | 1,145 | 1,085 | 880 | 2,455 | 133,319 | T442 Domination |
+
+These late totals include the consequences of different wars and eliminations, so they are not a laboratory measurement of production choice alone. The earlier direct evidence points in the same direction: by T150, flat 40 / 20 / 0 had produced 259 / 226 / 130 offensive units and completed 226 / 230 / 280 buildings. Flat 40 created more military pressure, but its later history consumed much more production in combat and retained no larger T350 combat army than flat 20. Flat 20 produced fewer offensive units but supported the strongest surviving economy and the earliest victory in this benchmark. Flat 0 devoted more early production to buildings but eventually lacked enough offensive pressure.
+
+An initially attractive `0 / 10 / 20 / 30 / 40 / 50 / 50` curve was repeated three times. All three runs remained identical in their aggregate history through T250: 57 cities, 732 population, 2,702 combined research, 608 current combat units, 583 produced offensive units, 495 completed buildings and 24,470 production lost in combat. Their later histories diverged into T500 Time, T474 Space and T391 Domination victories, confirming that final outcomes can vary greatly after small late branches even when the earlier policy effect is reproducible. More importantly, the low early nominal targets did not force peaceful development: ordinary war and military logic still produced almost the same T250 offensive count as flat 20, while the weaker Worker, building and population foundation compounded. Raising Modern/Future to 50 then produced roughly flat-40 quantities of attackers without reliably retaining a larger army. Replacing those final 50 values with 40 reduced later forced production but could not repair the already weak foundation; that isolated run reached Space on T495.
+
+The three strongest **tested** candidates retained for comparison on other save files are:
+
+1. **`20 / 20 / 20 / 30 / 35 / 40 / 40` — strongest single-run military/economic compromise so far.** It reproduced the 20/20/20/30 history through T200, then tested whether the Industrial jump directly from 30 to 40 was unnecessarily large. At T250 it had 59 cities, 847 population, 1,679 production, 3,030 research, 593 current combat units, 609 produced offensive units, 653 completed buildings and 26,754 production lost in combat. The repeated Industrial-40 curve had almost the same population, army and buildings at that checkpoint (846, 599 and 657), but only 1,558 production and 2,321 research. By T350, the 35 curve had 866 current combat units despite producing only 1,106 offensive units and losing 77,662 production in combat; the two Industrial-40 runs averaged about 848 current combat units after producing about 1,192 offensive units and losing about 86,179 production. This suggests that Industrial 35 retained military strength more efficiently while preserving more economic room. Its history then entered destructive late wars: by T400 the current combat army had fallen to 426 and cumulative combat loss had risen to 197,759 before the T422 Space victory. This reinforces the late-history variance caveat, and one run is not enough to rank the curve definitively above the repeated candidate.
+2. **`20 / 20 / 20 / 30 / 40 / 40 / 40` — strongest repeated military/economic compromise.** Its two repetitions exactly matched the successful flat-20 history through T150, then diverged from one another only on T260. At T250 both had 60 cities, 846 population, 1,558 production, 2,321 research, 599 current combat units, 627 produced offensive units, 657 completed buildings and 25,594 production lost in combat. Compared with flat 20, it therefore added military strength while preserving building output and improving population/production in the shared history; compared with flat 40, it retained a much stronger economy and lost less military production. The repetitions won Space on T373 and T426.
+3. **Flat `20 / 20 / 20 / 20 / 20 / 20 / 20` — strongest peaceful scaling and ordinary-strategy freedom in save file 461.** It had the best T350 population, research and completed-building totals, the lowest combat-production loss among the three flat baselines, and a T373 Space victory. Because this is only a minimum floor, flat 20 does not prevent personalities, wars, danger or composition needs from producing more units. Its weakness is that it applies no extra correction to the specifically observed late-era military shortfall.
+
+The tested `10 / 10 / 20 / 30 / 40 / 40 / 40` curve remains a useful early-freedom boundary even though it ranks below these three on save file 461. Ancient 10 behaved identically to Ancient 20 through T50. By T150 it had completed 255 buildings versus 230 for the 20/20 ramp, with 573 versus 480 production and almost the same offensive output (218 versus 226), showing that lower Classical pressure can fund a stronger early economy. That advantage did not persist in this history: by T250 it had 478 current combat units, 614 completed buildings and 36,750 production lost in combat, versus 599, 657 and 25,594 for the 20/20 ramp; it eventually won Space on T465. Preserving this much early strategic variety may still allow stronger mid/late comebacks on other maps, but save file 461 did not reward it overall.
+
+All 11 documented save-file 461 full autoplays completed normally without a crash, including repeated histories that diverged late, early victories and the run that reached the T500 Time limit. This is useful stability evidence for the era-indexed production floor and its diagnostics, although it does not by itself select a gameplay default. One or two additional starting saves should be compared before choosing release values; repeating the same start is useful for measuring late divergence, but does not replace testing different geography, neighbors and early strategic opportunities.
+
+### Save file 463: freer early production with the same strong late curve
+
+Save file 463 tested the leading curves on a different starting position and clarified why a low early floor can be useful rather than merely permissive. AI personalities, wars, danger, military composition and other ordinary production logic generally already create enough early units. Enforcing additional militarization during the Ancient and Classical eras can instead displace Settlers, Workers, population growth and buildings whose returns compound through the rest of the game; it can also suppress AI personalities and strategies that need a different early balance or could otherwise recover strongly later. Conversely, KI#197.9 empirically found very low offensive production especially late in the game. By then, mature empires have less remaining time for new infrastructure to repay its cost, while military strength is directly needed to conquer, resist rivals, deny imminent victories and finish the game. This supports a deliberately progressive floor rather than assuming that the same percentage is efficient in every era.
+
+The `20 / 20 / 20 / 30 / 35 / 40 / 40` and `20 / 20 / 20 / 30 / 40 / 40 / 40` runs remained exactly identical through T200, giving a useful second comparison of the Industrial setting. At T250, Industrial 35 had slightly more population, research, current combat units and completed buildings while producing fewer total units and losing less production in combat. By T350 it had 1,069 population, 2,624 production, 5,201 research, 763 current combat units, 1,337 completed buildings and 82,145 production lost in combat, versus Industrial 40's 860, 1,801, 2,839, 637, 1,255 and 111,596 respectively. Different wars magnified the later gap, but the direct floor diagnostics also recorded 234 Industrial-era choices at 35 versus 281 at 40. Together with save file 461, this makes 35 the stronger tested Industrial default so far: 40 forced more production without producing a larger retained army or stronger economy.
+
+The additional `0 / 10 / 20 / 30 / 35 / 40 / 40` run preserved the promising late curve while testing substantially more Ancient/Classical freedom. Zero in the Ancient era disables only this extra floor; it does not disable ordinary military production. Its early and middle checkpoints compared with the otherwise identical `20 / 20 / 20 / 30 / 35 / 40 / 40` policy as follows:
+
+| Turn and curve | Cities | Population | Production | Research | Current combat units | Workers | All units produced | Buildings completed | Production lost in combat |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| T50, `0 / 10 / 20` start | 18 | 71 | 99 | 223 | 52 | 22 | 79 | 6 | 254 |
+| T50, `20 / 20 / 20` start | 15 | 72 | 97 | 217 | 56 | 19 | 72 | 6 | 189 |
+| T150, `0 / 10 / 20` start | 55 | 463 | 655 | 933 | 366 | 97 | 645 | 324 | 2,643 |
+| T150, `20 / 20 / 20` start | 55 | 424 | 588 | 768 | 359 | 103 | 653 | 279 | 3,205 |
+| T250, `0 / 10 / 20` start | 63 | 856 | 1,386 | 2,903 | 641 | 128 | 1,574 | 848 | 33,344 |
+| T250, `20 / 20 / 20` start | 64 | 846 | 1,413 | 3,348 | 532 | 133 | 1,567 | 749 | 38,357 |
+| T300, `0 / 10 / 20` start | 64 | 1,016 | 2,321 | 4,625 | 751 | 141 | 2,027 | 1,119 | 63,928 |
+| T300, `20 / 20 / 20` start | 63 | 954 | 1,841 | 4,066 | 599 | 147 | 2,071 | 989 | 73,959 |
+
+The freer start therefore did not leave the AIs defenseless. At T50 it had founded 3 more cities and built 3 more Workers while retaining only 4 fewer combat units. By T150 it had more population, production, research, buildings and combat units with lower combat loss; by T300 it still had 152 more current combat units despite the lower early floor. Its history became more destructive after T300, so the 20-start curve again had the stronger aggregate economy and army at T350, but Ethiopia nevertheless won Space on T389 as both the top-score and top-power civilization. This is strong evidence that early freedom can preserve rather than sacrifice later military strength, although the immediate divergence means that one history cannot prove it will always outperform 20/20.
+
+For comparison, flat 20 had the strongest immediate T250 army and lowest combat loss in save file 463, then weakened during its later wars before Willem won Space on T445. Industrial 35 reached an Ethiopian Space victory on T435, while Industrial 40 reached the same victory type only on T460. Final victories remain highly history-dependent, but the current cross-save finalists are therefore `0 / 10 / 20 / 30 / 35 / 40 / 40` for greater early strategic freedom and `20 / 20 / 20 / 30 / 35 / 40 / 40` for a safer early military floor. Industrial 40 is no longer a leading candidate unless another save exposes a weakness at 35; flat 20 remains a useful control for whether the late ramp is genuinely needed.
+
+The civilization-specific building rework also remained active under every curve: Willem completed exactly 7 Netherlands Beurs in each of the four save-file 463 runs. The freer curve completed them on T229, T264, T310, T313, T334, T336 and T339; the other curves changed their timing but not the final count. In the flat-20 history, Willem launched on T433 and reached Space on T445, one turn before Ethiopia's later but faster spaceship would arrive. The Beurs likely supported his economy, but the record does not establish that they caused the victory.
+
+All four save-file 463 autoplays also completed normally without a crash. Together with save file 461, all 15 full tuning runs completed successfully, including reruns whose histories diverged substantially. The next useful comparison is the two current finalists on a different and preferably larger or strategically different map.
+
+### Save file 468: the two finalists on a Huge map
+
+Save file 468 compared the two finalists on a 94x64 Huge Pangaea with 16 civilizations. Both histories were exactly identical through T50; the Ancient 20 floor made only 10 direct production choices before the histories first diverged by T60. This is important context: Ancient 0 did not remove a broad military program, but allowed a small number of cities to follow their ordinary production priorities instead. Subsequent differences include compounding production choices, wars and other random history, so no late aggregate should be attributed to the floor alone.
+
+| Turn and curve | Cities | Population | Production | Research | Current combat units | Workers | All units produced | Buildings completed | Production lost in combat |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| T150, `0 / 10 / 20` start | 79 | 700 | 876 | 1,420 | 551 | 147 | 1,001 | 411 | 5,334 |
+| T150, `20 / 20 / 20` start | 79 | 653 | 882 | 1,783 | 550 | 146 | 1,044 | 413 | 7,459 |
+| T200, `0 / 10 / 20` start | 84 | 1,042 | 1,481 | 2,574 | 757 | 175 | 1,740 | 723 | 27,578 |
+| T200, `20 / 20 / 20` start | 86 | 1,024 | 1,349 | 2,207 | 715 | 174 | 1,686 | 697 | 25,928 |
+| T250, `0 / 10 / 20` start | 90 | 1,250 | 2,206 | 3,657 | 761 | 206 | 2,459 | 1,034 | 75,405 |
+| T250, `20 / 20 / 20` start | 95 | 1,309 | 2,400 | 3,227 | 888 | 199 | 2,460 | 1,015 | 65,281 |
+| T300, `0 / 10 / 20` start | 91 | 1,373 | 3,080 | 4,602 | 822 | 217 | 3,288 | 1,346 | 155,505 |
+| T300, `20 / 20 / 20` start | 92 | 1,328 | 2,450 | 2,968 | 809 | 221 | 3,177 | 1,273 | 147,680 |
+
+The Huge-map comparison was mixed in a useful way rather than showing one universally larger empire. The 20-start history had more immediate military strength, cities and population at T250, whereas the freer history generally completed more buildings and by T300 had more population, production, research and current combat units. The freer curve again demonstrated that less forced early military production does not imply a weak later army. Conversely, the 20-start curve remained a credible safer policy for consistently increasing early military pressure.
+
+The most informative difference was strategic. Cyrus of Persia won both histories, but used two different victory paths. With the freer curve, Cyrus progressed from Culture stage 1 on T250 to stage 2 on T300, stage 3 on T340 and stage 4 by T450. He deliberately used a 100% Culture slider on T350 and T460, and won Culture on T466 with his three cities at 49,546, 45,340 and 45,087 culture against the 45,000 requirement. With the 20-start curve, the same Cyrus considered Culture early but later abandoned it, reached Space stage 4, launched on T481 and won Space on T491. The freer history therefore produced a valid alternative victory 25 turns earlier; it was not accidental passive culture accumulation. This supports preserving early strategic flexibility rather than forcing every AI toward the same opening balance.
+
+### Current conclusion and preferred default
+
+The current preferred curve is **`0 / 10 / 20 / 30 / 35 / 40 / 40`**. These values are minimum shares of empire base production currently committed to `UNITAI_ATTACK` / `UNITAI_ATTACK_CITY`, not unit-count quotas or caps on ordinary military production. Personalities, war, danger, defense and composition logic can always choose more military production when needed.
+
+- **Ancient 0:** apply no extra floor while Settlers, Workers, growth and foundational buildings have their longest compounding value; ordinary AI logic still produces military units.
+- **Classical 10:** add only a light backstop while preserving different personalities and economic, expansion or military openings.
+- **Medieval 20:** begin a meaningful minimum as empires consolidate, without yet imposing the late-game correction.
+- **Renaissance 30:** deliberately increase pressure where the previously observed military underproduction becomes increasingly relevant.
+- **Industrial 35:** apply a strong correction while avoiding the tested inefficiency of jumping directly to 40.
+- **Modern/Future 40:** strongly correct the severe late military shortfall when mature empires have less time for new infrastructure to repay its cost and greater urgency to conquer, defend, deny rival victories or finish their own winning strategy.
+
+This progression is intended to fix the empirically very low mid/late offensive production without sacrificing the early growth, economic scaling and strategic variety that can create a stronger late-game civilization. It is not claimed to be a mathematically universal optimum, but two different comparison saves now favor the freer curve overall, neither exposed an early military collapse, and both retained substantial later military strength. The `20 / 20 / 20 / 30 / 35 / 40 / 40` curve remains the tested conservative alternative if future evidence shows that a particular map or ruleset needs more early pressure.
+
+Every one of the 17 documented tuning autoplays was started at T0 and requested to run continuously through the T501/end-of-game boundary. Every run completed normally until an earlier victory or the Time limit, without a single crash, including repeated histories that diverged substantially and both 16-civilization Huge-map runs. This provides strong stability evidence for the era-indexed implementation and its diagnostics, separately from the gameplay-balance conclusion.
+
