-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6094+) **Optionally increase tradeoff between offensive unit production maximizing (but too undevelope to scale militarily or economically into late game) and economy maximizing (but militarily weak and likely to die or lose later) trade-off so it is broadly closer to an efficient middle-ground:** the old AdvCiv-SAS 5055 stable release could produce very large AI armies, while later releases generally focused more and preferred economic efficiency instead, which however produced substantially fewer military units and could also make games less threatening and easier to win, especially late game. To address this, offensive military production is now broadly tunable through `SAS_AI_CHOOSE_PRODUCTION_MIN_OFFENSIVE_PRODUCTION_CAPACITY_PERCENT`, which sets the minimum share of the empire's **base production currently committed to `UNITAI_ATTACK` / `UNITAI_ATTACK_CITY` units** before opportunistic wonders and the broader later building choices, but after existing Worker, Settler, Explorer, panic, financial, culture and other earlier priority logic (so essential economic and survival production can still take precedence, even if set to max `100`): `0` disables the feature; the current middle-ground default of `40` significantly increases offensive production while still leaving enough room for infrastructure and economic growth to scale the empire; values up to `100` apply stronger military-production pressure, but can overcommit production to units and therefore do not necessarily produce a larger or stronger late-game army (possibly even less due to lower production or economy scaling late game). A same-start save file 460 BBAI and SASGameRecord logging comparison illustrates the trade-off: with `0` / `40` / `100`, the 11 civilizations (excluding Barbarians) had built 737 / 952 / 985 combat units by turn 250, and 987 / 1,340 / 1,340 by turn 300, so `40` captured nearly all of the additional military output of `100` while leaving substantially more room for infrastructure and economic scaling. The target deliberately uses **production capacity rather than a fixed unit count, era quota or military-upkeep target**, so high-production cities contribute proportionally more and the system naturally scales with the empire. The current feature improves military output significantly especially late game where it was too low, but does not reproduce the exceptionally high AdvCiv-SAS 5055 military-production levels (usually of ~200 units by T250-T300 and ~300 units by T300-T400) in amount or in consistency, so further AI-production tuning may continue. Note: as of now the military production increases mostly effective mid-late game with a value of 40% vs before, which is when output was lowest so desired effect. See [KI#197.9](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1979---improved-healthy-ai-empires-could-still-devote-too-little-productive-capacity-to-offensive-units-even-after-buildunitprob-and-spending-limit-fixes).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6094+) **AI offensive production rises progressively by era, preserving early growth and strategic variety while correcting weak middle and late armies:** the old AdvCiv-SAS 5055 stable release could produce very large AI armies, while later releases generally favored economic efficiency but produced substantially fewer offensive units, especially late in the game. AdvCiv-SAS therefore measures the share of each empire's **base production currently committed to `UNITAI_ATTACK` / `UNITAI_ATTACK_CITY` units** and, when it is below an XML-tunable minimum (the `SAS_AI_CHOOSE_PRODUCTION_MIN_OFFENSIVE_PRODUCTION_CAPACITY` defines), asks an eligible city to help fill the shortfall before opportunistic wonders and broader later building choices. This is a production-capacity floor rather than a fixed unit count, unit-production cap or military-upkeep target, so productive cities contribute proportionally more and personalities, wars, danger, defense and composition logic can always produce more units when needed. Worker, Settler, Explorer, panic, financial, culture and other earlier priorities still take precedence. The default Ancient-to-Future curve as of now is **`0 / 10 / 20 / 30 / 35 / 40 / 40`**: it leaves the Ancient era entirely to ordinary AI logic, adds only light Classical pressure, then deliberately strengthens from the Medieval era onward as foundational growth and infrastructure have less remaining time to compound and military urgency increases. Extensive same-save comparisons showed why a flat target was inferior: high early pressure could displace growth and buildings without retaining a larger later army, while insufficient late pressure preserved an economy that could not defend itself or convert its strength into victory. SASGameRecord and BBAI Logging in save files 463 and 468 both retained substantial later armies with the freer early curve; it won on T389 versus T435 and on T466 versus T491 respectively, with the Huge-map T466 result also allowing Cyrus to pursue a deliberate Cultural victory rather than the Space path followed by the same civilization under the more conservative curve. Industrial 35 also retained military strength more efficiently than 40 in save files 461 and 463. The seven era values remain independently tunable for different player preferences or rulesets. See [KI#197.9](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1979---improved-healthy-ai-empires-could-still-devote-too-little-productive-capacity-to-offensive-units-even-after-buildunitprob-and-spending-limit-fixes) and [KI#197.10](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#19710---improved--tuned-an-era-progressive-offensive-production-floor-preserves-early-ai-strategies-while-correcting-weak-middle-and-late-armies).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6097+) **Risky AI stack attacks can use obsolete units as expendables / cannon fodder before current units:** BBAI attack-order logging in save file 460 showed the SAS low-power selector could choose the first unit to attack to be obsolete unit, only for inherited Base AdvCiv/K-Mod sacrifice logic to replace it with a current unit first attacking instead; one sample had 189 obsolete-to-current replacements, including an obsolete Archer at 10% winning odds being replaced by a current (plus much stronger) Horse Archer. To fix this, when the current unit would already take a risky attack, the AI can instead spend a tactically suitable obsolete unit first as cannon fodder/expendable, potentially weakening the defender while preserving current stronger units for safer follow-up attacks. Very safe current attacks (90%+ as of now) remain untouched, and XML-tunable sacrifice-value, tactical-role and odds safeguards prevent blanket weakest-unit-first behavior. Confirming runs showed expendables often damaged defenders or withdrew; in one sequence, obsolete attackers improved a preserved Mechanized Infantry's odds from 36% to 99%, after which it won without damage. This also helps clear weak obsolete excess naturally instead of keeping it for unit support or often poor-value future upgrades. See [KI#200](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#200---improved-inherited-base-advcivk-mod-sacrifice-selection-could-spend-current-units-before-sas-defined-obsolete-expendables-during-risky-ai-stack-attacks).
